  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "As discussed in detail in Chapter 23, for species with maternal care, the phenotype of an individual can depend on both genetic and environmental components of maternal effects, in addition to the individual's direct additive genetic value and a random environmental deviation. As first pointed out by Quaas and Pollak (1981), Willham's (1963) model for maternal effects is readily incorporated into a mixed-model framework, allowing the estimation of breeding values as well as maternal genetic and environmental effects. Here we simply consider the animal-model framework, although application of the reduced animal model is also straightforward (Mrode 1996). The structure of the model is very similar to that provided above for dominance and for repeated measures, the main difference being the addition of a third vector of random effects.",
      "source_page": 785,
      "printed_page": 765,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter26:p029:b3",
        "Genetics_chapter26:p029:b4"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        56.0,
        296.0,
        1018.0,
        665.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        785
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          64.0,
          253.0,
          276.0,
          288.0
        ],
        [
          56.0,
          296.0,
